strawberry.tools.merge_types only merges fields. The description, directives and extend flag of the types being merged are dropped:
import strawberry
from strawberry.schema_directive import Location
from strawberry.tools import merge_types


@strawberry.schema_directive(locations=[Location.OBJECT])
class CacheControl:
    max_age: int


@strawberry.type(description="Product queries", directives=[CacheControl(max_age=60)])
class ProductQueries:
    @strawberry.field
    def product(self) -> str: ...


@strawberry.type
class AccountQueries:
    @strawberry.field
    def account(self) -> str: ...


Query = merge_types("Query", (ProductQueries, AccountQueries))
type Query {
  product: String!
  account: String!
}
This is consistent with inheritance (a @strawberry.type subclass doesn't inherit its base's description or directives either), and the docstring describes merge_types as "essentially the same as" subclassing. But it's easy to be surprised by it: you put a directive on a type, merge it, and the directive silently disappears.
We hit this in the federation schema, which uses merge_types to add _service and _entities to the user's Query. As a result, directives and the description on the root Query type were dropped (being fixed separately, by passing them on explicitly).
Proposal
Merge type-level metadata by default, with flags to opt out, for example:
Query = merge_types(
    "Query",
    (ProductQueries, AccountQueries),
    merge_directives=True,  # default
    merge_des

